### My answer to the prompt

Do you mean automatic cleanup of a normal linked list, or a tracing garbage collector that also handles arbitrary reference cycles? For a normal singly linked list, C++ smart pointers provide automatic cleanup through ownership—no manual `delete` is needed.

Here is a C++14 example:

```cpp
#include <memory>
#include <utility>

class LinkedList {
    struct Node {
        int value;
        std::unique_ptr<Node> next;

        explicit Node(int v) : value(v) {}
    };

    std::unique_ptr<Node> head;

public:
    LinkedList() = default;
    LinkedList(const LinkedList&) = delete;
    LinkedList& operator=(const LinkedList&) = delete;

    void push_front(int value) {
        auto node = std::make_unique<Node>(value);
        node->next = std::move(head);
        head = std::move(node);
    }

    void pop_front() {
        if (!head) return;

        auto removed = std::move(head);
        head = std::move(removed->next);
        // Only the removed node is destroyed here.
    }

    void clear() {
        while (head) pop_front();
    }

    ~LinkedList() {
        clear(); // Iterative cleanup avoids deep recursive destruction.
    }
};

int main() {
    LinkedList list;
    list.push_front(10);
    list.push_front(20);
    list.pop_front();
} // Remaining nodes are automatically freed.
```

This is **RAII-based memory management, not tracing garbage collection**. Each node exclusively owns its successor. If you need circular lists or shared node ownership, the ownership design needs additional consideration.

