## My answer to the prompt

Treat each existing connected component as one vertex. Let its weight be the price of its cheapest house.

Choose the globally cheapest house `s`. Connect `s` to the cheapest house in every component other than its own.

**Why this is optimal:** If there are `k` components, positive road costs mean an optimal solution can be a tree with `k - 1` new roads. Root any such tree at the component containing `s`. For each other component `C`, the edge to its parent costs at least

\[
\minPrice(C) + price(s).
\]

The proposed construction achieves this lower bound for every non-root component, so its total cost is minimal.

Use a disjoint-set union structure (DSU) to find the components.

**Complexity:** \(O((n+m)\alpha(n))\) time and \(O(n)\) extra space.

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

struct DSU {
    vector<int> parent, sz;

    explicit DSU(int n) : parent(n + 1), sz(n + 1, 1) {
        iota(parent.begin(), parent.end(), 0);
    }

    int find(int v) {
        return parent[v] == v ? v : parent[v] = find(parent[v]);
    }

    void unite(int u, int v) {
        u = find(u);
        v = find(v);
        if (u == v) return;
        if (sz[u] < sz[v]) swap(u, v);
        parent[v] = u;
        sz[u] += sz[v];
    }
};

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int n, m;
    cin >> n >> m;

    vector<int> price(n + 1);
    int s = 1;
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
        cin >> price[i];
        if (price[i] < price[s]) s = i;
    }

    DSU dsu(n);
    for (int i = 0; i < m; ++i) {
        int u, v;
        cin >> u >> v;
        dsu.unite(u, v);
    }

    vector<int> best(n + 1, -1);
    for (int i = 1; i <= n; ++i) {
        int root = dsu.find(i);
        if (best[root] == -1 || price[i] < price[best[root]]) {
            best[root] = i;
        }
    }

    int rootS = dsu.find(s);
    vector<pair<int, int>> roads;

    for (int root = 1; root <= n; ++root) {
        if (best[root] != -1 && root != rootS) {
            roads.emplace_back(s, best[root]);
        }
    }

    cout << roads.size() << '\n';
    for (auto [u, v] : roads) {
        cout << u << ' ' << v << '\n';
    }
}
```

If the city is already connected, this prints `0`. Any optimal set of roads is acceptable; it need not match the sample exactly.

