If you mean “execute a callback and return either its value or the error it throws,” use a discriminated union:

```ts
export type Result<T, E = unknown> =
  | { readonly ok: true; readonly value: T }
  | { readonly ok: false; readonly error: E };

export function tryCatch<T>(fn: () => T): Result<T> {
  try {
    return { ok: true, value: fn() };
  } catch (error: unknown) {
    return { ok: false, error };
  }
}

export async function tryCatchAsync<T>(
  fn: () => T,
): Promise<Result<Awaited<T>>> {
  try {
    return { ok: true, value: await fn() };
  } catch (error: unknown) {
    return { ok: false, error };
  }
}
```

Usage:

```ts
const result = tryCatch(() => JSON.parse('{"name":"Ada"}') as unknown);

if (result.ok) {
  console.log(result.value);
} else {
  console.error(result.error);
}
```

Errors are `unknown` because JavaScript can throw any value. Use `tryCatchAsync` for promise-returning callbacks: the synchronous version cannot catch a later promise rejection.

These helpers execute the callback immediately. If you instead want a wrapper that returns a new function preserving the original parameters, that requires a different signature.

