## My solution

The original order matters: we cannot necessarily keep the largest elements.

Let the final length be
\[
r=(n-1)\bmod k+1,
\qquad q=\frac{n-r}{k}.
\]

### Reachable final arrays

Suppose the surviving elements have original indices \(p_1<\cdots<p_r\), using 1-based indexing.

An operation cannot cross an element that survives. Therefore, every gap before, between, and after the survivors must contain a multiple of \(k\) elements. Equivalently,
\[
p_j=j+b_jk,\qquad 0\le b_1\le\cdots\le b_r\le q.
\]

This condition is also sufficient: delete each gap in chunks of length \(k\).

### Checking a candidate answer

For a threshold \(x\), mark an element as good if it is at least \(x\). The lower median is at least \(x\) exactly when at least
\[
\left\lfloor\frac r2\right\rfloor+1
\]
surviving elements are good.

Process blocks numbered \(0,\ldots,q\). The \(j\)-th survivor can only be the \(j\)-th element of one of these blocks.

Maintain `dp[j]`, the maximum number of good elements achievable after choosing the first `j` survivors. For each block, update `j` in increasing order:
```cpp
dp[j] = max(dp[j], dp[j - 1] + (a[block * k + j - 1] >= x));
```

Increasing order permits several survivors to come from the same block. Keeping the previous value permits skipping the current block for that survivor.

Binary search finds the largest feasible threshold.

### C++17

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int t;
    cin >> t;

    while (t--) {
        int n, k;
        cin >> n >> k;

        vector<int> a(n);
        for (int &v : a) cin >> v;

        int r = (n - 1) % k + 1;
        int q = (n - r) / k;
        int required = r / 2 + 1;

        vector<int> dp(r + 1);

        auto feasible = [&](int x) {
            fill(dp.begin(), dp.end(), -1000000000);
            dp[0] = 0;

            for (int block = 0; block <= q; ++block) {
                for (int j = 1; j <= r; ++j) {
                    int good = (a[block * k + j - 1] >= x);
                    dp[j] = max(dp[j], dp[j - 1] + good);
                }
            }

            return dp[r] >= required;
        };

        int low = *min_element(a.begin(), a.end());
        int high = *max_element(a.begin(), a.end());

        while (low < high) {
            int mid = low + (high - low + 1) / 2;
            if (feasible(mid))
                low = mid;
            else
                high = mid - 1;
        }

        cout << low << '\n';
    }
}
```

**Correctness:** The gap characterization describes exactly the reachable final arrays. The DP considers precisely all non-decreasing choices of block numbers and maximizes their number of good elements. Its feasibility test therefore matches the median condition exactly. Since feasibility is monotonic in the threshold, binary search returns the optimal median.

**Complexity:** Each check processes \((q+1)r\le qk+r=n\) positions. Total time is \(O(n\log V)\), where \(V=\max a_i\), and space is \(O(n)\).

