## My answer

The cost equals the sum of prefix balances: a matched pair at positions \(l,r\) contributes one to every balance from \(l\) through \(r-1\), totaling \(r-l\).

For any valid restoration:
- Each odd-position prefix has balance at least \(1\).
- Each even position containing `(` has balance at least \(2\).
- Each even position containing `)` has balance at least \(0\).

All these bounds can be achieved simultaneously. At each missing position, insert `(` if the current balance is zero; otherwise insert `)`. The balance after each odd position becomes \(1\), and after each even position becomes either \(0\) or \(2\). The guaranteed existence of a restoration implies that the last character is `)`, so the final balance is zero.

Thus,
\[
\boxed{\text{minimum cost}=\frac n2+2\cdot\text{number of fixed opening brackets}}.
\]

```cpp
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    ios::sync_with_stdio(false);
    cin.tie(nullptr);

    int t;
    cin >> t;

    while (t--) {
        int n;
        string s;
        cin >> n >> s;

        long long answer = n / 2;
        for (int i = 1; i < n; i += 2) {
            if (s[i] == '(') answer += 2;
        }

        cout << answer << '\n';
    }
}
```

**Complexity:** \(O(n)\) time per test case and \(O(1)\) auxiliary space beyond the input string.

